## Installation
If you’re intending to use the pre-compiled binaries, like {{model.binariesSentence}},
then installation on your system is a matter of copying the libraries, and 
binaries to your preferred install location, ensuring the libraries can be found 
at runtime, and the binaries are on your PATH.

If you’re intending to also use the C API, then you need to ensure the header 
files and also installed somewhere your build system can find them. We do not 
currently recommend using the C++ API with the pre-compiled `libsaxonc` in case 
of ABI conflict, for that you can compile `libsaxonc` yourself.

If you’re intending to compile `libsaxonc` yourself, then what you need to do 
will depend on your project and its build system. We provide out-of-the-box 
compatibility with CMake, but using other systems should be straightforward.

### Installing the files
We recommend copying the contents of the `{{model.SaxonCCorePackage}}` and `{{model.SaxonCPackage}}`
directories within the zip to a location like /opt/saxonica/. You would then end 
up with a structure like /opt/saxonica/lib, /opt/saxonica/bin, and 
/opt/saxonica/include.

For example:

	mkdir -p /opt/saxonica
	cp -a {{model.SaxonCCorePackage}}/* /opt/saxonica/
	cp -a {{model.SaxonCPackage}}/* /opt/saxonica/

If you’re only going to use the pre-compiled `libsaxonc-core`, you may only need 
to copy the contents of `${model.SaxonCCorePackage}`, depending on your build system.

### Set the {{model.runtimeLinkerVar}} and PATH environment variables
The {{model.runtimeLinkerVar}} variable must be set to the location of the lib directory
containing the SaxonC libraries. For instance:

	export {{model.runtimeLinkerVar}}="/opt/saxonica/lib:${{model.runtimeLinkerVar}}"

To run the {{model.editionBinariesSentence}} binaries without referencing their full
path, the PATH variable can be set. For instance:

	export PATH="/opt/saxonica/bin:$PATH"
